As a relative noob, my $0.02 is probably worth less than its face
value, but:

On Jun 27, 9:39 pm, Carl <carlhnel...@gmail.com> wrote:
> I opened the door to the chamber on the second Sokoban level and sent
> in my large dog first.  I had to shuffle a bit because I wanted to
> tame a pony that I knew was inside and a hostile dwarf entered the
> hallway armed with a wand of lightning.

From what I have gathered, as with ranged weapons, monsters won't zap
offensive wands when they are immediately adjacent to you (this may
not be actually true, so if someone with more experience knows
different please jump in). My standard Sokoban strategy is to stand
out in the hallway on the square diagonally adjacent to the doorway
until everything inside the room is dead; in this position the only
two squares within line-of-sight that the monsters in the room can
access are adjacent to you, like so (assuming two mummies on those
spots):

|..........|      |.........|
|....|.....----------------M|
|....|....................@M|
-----------------------------

If I have a hard gem or suitable ring (or wand with charges to spare),
I'll engrave Elbereth on that square before opening the door,
especially if I don't have a key/lockpick/credit card. (I avoid
kicking in the door if possible, since if the door is intact, with a
little luck you can keep everything bottled up in the doorway and
slaughter the occupants one at a time.) This also offers the option of
taking a break, by dropping some junk on the square and moving away
(although you will be susceptible to ranged weapons while in the
hallway, and watch out for scuffing the engraving).

If I have a key or lockpick and there's something in the room I don't
want to deal with but I think my pet can handle, after shoving the pet
into the room, I'll try to close and lock the door until I don't hear
any more noises (use Elbereth to scare away any monsters in the
doorway, then kick away or pick up any junk blocking it).

> The scroll of earth might have been the
> better choice since blessed would not have hit me, uncursed would have
> hit me for d20 (IIRC) but cursed would have hit me for d20 and left me
> exposed.

You have a dog; he'll tell you which if any of the scrolls are cursed.
The boulder from a cursed scroll ends up on the same square you are
on, so even using a cursed scroll and then moving in the direction
away from the gnome would have blocked the hallway (if you could stay
alive for the two moves this would take).

